// Returning an `arc` FIELD, the shape that produced a heap-use-after-free.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `99d2971` fixed exactly this: `return s.name` for an
// `arc` field emitted `return s->name;` without a retain, so the caller
// released a reference nobody had taken for it. docs/OWNERSHIP.md R11 release
// rule 3 is categorical, a returned `arc` comes back already retained and the
// caller releases it, and that was honoured for a returned LOCAL only.
//
// The defect passed `cell check`, compiled clean at `-std=c11 -Wall -Wextra
// -Werror`, and was found only when a reviewer ran the program under
// AddressSanitizer BY HAND. Nothing in the corpus reproduced it: measured at
// `99d2971^`, every runnable example was ASan-clean, so a sanitizer stage over
// the corpus as it stood would have found nothing either. A sanitizer is only
// as good as the programs it runs, so the program has to exist. This is that
// program, and `tools/check.sh` runs it under ASan.
//
// `arc.cell` does not cover it, and its near miss is instructive: it declares
// this same `Session` with an `arc name` field and then never constructs one
// and never returns a field, so the whole field path went unexercised while
// looking covered.
//
// WHY TWO CALLS TO `peek`, AND NOT ONE. This is the load-bearing detail. With
// ONE alias the arithmetic balances by accident and the program is clean even
// when the retain is missing: `label` holds one reference, the struct literal
// clones a second, the unretained alias is dropped (2 to 1) and `label` is
// dropped (1 to 0), and nothing touches the box afterwards. Measured: that
// version exits 0 under ASan against the broken compiler, so it would have
// pinned nothing. A SECOND alias takes the count one drop too far: the two
// aliases release 2 to 1 to 0, freeing the box while `label` is still live,
// and `label`'s own drop is then a heap-use-after-free inside cell_arc_drop.
// Measured against the compiler at `99d2971^`: exit 134, "AddressSanitizer:
// heap-use-after-free". Do not "simplify" this file down to one call.
//
// The struct's own reference is never released, because R11 row 2 discloses
// that a struct holding an `arc` field is never dropped. That leak is a known,
// measured gap with its own fixture in examples/leaks/, not something this
// file is trying to fix; leaking is the safe side of the error, and the point
// here is that the program no longer DANGLES.
//
// The printed value is `s.id`, a plain 7, and it is deliberately not the thing
// under test. What this file measures is whether the program is memory-safe,
// which is a fact about how it dies rather than about what it prints, and
// `tools/check.sh`'s sanitizer stage is what reads it. The number is here so
// that the answer-comparison stage has something to pin as well.
//
// LLVM and MLIR refuse this file, identically and on purpose, the same way
// they refuse arc.cell: `arc` has no retain and release insertion in either
// backend. The gate's agreement stage pins that refusal.
//
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: 7

pub struct Session {
    arc name: String
    copy id: Int
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

// The returned `arc` place. `s` is a `shared` borrow, so this function does
// not own the field it is handing out, and the caller is going to release
// whatever it receives: the reference has to be created here or it does not
// exist. `cell_arc_clone(s->name)` is the correct emission.
pub fn peek(shared s: Session) -> arc String {
    return s.name
}

pub fn main() {
    let arc label = "session"
    let owned s = Session { name: arc label, id: 7 }

    // Two aliases, for the reason the header explains at length. One is not
    // enough to make the missing retain observable.
    let arc one = peek(shared s)
    let arc two = peek(shared s)

    print_int(s.id)
}
